speechtext:
I did not wish to interrupt my hon. friend in his speech, because I noticed that he has given careful consideration to this subject; but apparently he was under a misapprehension and thought that it was another resolution that was under consideration. The resolution now before the House has nothing whatever to do, more than the gathering of information, with the suspension of the coasting laws. The objections of my hon. friend would come quite correctly in the consideration of the next bill to be placed before the House. All we are doing now is to provide publicity; so that the shipper will be compelled to publish his rates, and	to publish the space	available;
If the Board of Grain Commissioners so determine, they may fix a maximum rate. I will not follow this any further than to make this statement. The hon. member for South Cape Breton (Mr. Carroll) referred to the enor-mouse increase in the export of grain through the port of Montreal, and he objected very strenuously to Canada permitting American ships to come into our waters. May I say right now that most of the grain ^ going through the port of Montreal comes in now on American ships?
Lake Grain Rates

I remember distinctly that last session, I think it was in the debate upon the Address, two distinct charges were made from the benches to my right that the government had been returned to power by cer-tan interests in this country. Those charges were allowed to go unchallenged. I think it is the rule in this House to permit general charges of that character as long as they are not personal. I do not know how this applies. On page 63 of Beauchesne's Parliamentary Rules and Forms I find this paragraph :
If a member should say nothing disrespectful to the House or the Chair, or personally opprobrious to other members, or in violation of other rules of the House he may state whatever he may think fit in debate, however offensive it may be to the feelings, or injurious to the character of individuals;
There is nothing personal in the statement of the hon. member for Southeast Grey but the general charge she made has been interpreted as being something personal. If a charge is personal it is against the rules of the House but that cannot be said of a general statement such as the hon. member made.
